# 02 File Navigation and Project Management *file-nav*

Mastering file navigation is the first step toward an efficient
editing workflow. This tutorial covers the full path — from directory
browsing and fuzzy search to buffer management — for finding files
in your project.

## 1. File Tree (Neotree) *neotree*

Press `<F4>` to toggle the Neotree file tree panel. This gives you a
directory-structure view of your project, perfect for browsing and
organizing files:

- `j`/`k` to move up and down; Enter to open a file or expand a
  directory
- `?` to show Neotree help — there are many useful keybindings
- Press `<F4>` again to close the panel

TIP: Use the file tree when you roughly know where a file lives. If
you know the filename but not the location, Telescope (next section)
is faster.

## 2. Fuzzy Search (Telescope) *telescope*

Telescope is the universal search tool in this configuration:

- `<C-f>`: Search files — filters by filename in real time
- `<C-p>`: Open Telescope main menu — choose a search mode
- `<C-x><C-b>` or `<C-c>`: Show buffer list — switch between already
  open files

Telescope opens a search box at the bottom of the window. Just start
typing — it supports fuzzy matching, so a few letters of the
filename is enough.

NOTE: `<C-f>` is your daily driver for project files. `<C-x><C-b>`
is great when you need to switch between already open files.

## 3. mini.files File Manager *minifiles*

Press `<C-x><C-f>` to open mini.files. Unlike Telescope's "search
and select" model, mini.files lets you browse the directory tree:

- Use `h`/`j`/`k`/`l` to move focus
- Press Enter to open a file or enter a directory
- Press `-` to go up one directory level
- Supports file operations: copy, move, rename, delete

**When to use it?** When you need to browse the directory structure,
manage files (copy/move/rename), or you're not sure of the exact
filename.

TIP: `<C-x>f` (Pick mode) is best when you know the filename.
`<C-x><C-f>` (mini.files) is best when you want to browse. They
complement each other — use the right tool for the moment.

## 4. Oil File Manager *oil*

Press `<leader>O` (Space then capital O) to open Oil. Unlike
mini.files, Oil presents a directory as a regular text buffer — you
edit the file list like editing text:

- Delete a file line → deletes the file
- Rename a file line → renames the file
- Add a new line → creates a new file

This unique approach to file management is especially handy for batch
operations.

## 5. Buffer Management *buffers*

In Neovim, a "buffer" is an open file. This config provides rich
buffer management keybindings:

- `<S-h>` / `<S-l>`: Switch to previous/next buffer
- `[b` / `]b`: Cycle to previous/next buffer
- `[B` / `]B`: Move current buffer one position left/right
- `<leader>1`-`<leader>9`: Jump to buffer number 1-9
- `<leader>bp`: Pin/unpin the current buffer
- `<leader>bP`: Close all unpinned buffers
- `<leader>br`: Close all buffers to the right
- `<leader>bl`: Close all buffers to the left

**Recommended workflow:** Use `<S-h>`/`<S-l>` to flip through files.
Pin important buffers (`<leader>bp`), then close everything else in
one go (`<leader>bP`) when you need to clean up.

## 6. Bufferline Enhancements *bufferline*

Bufferline shows all open buffers as tabs at the top of the window,
with extra navigation options:

- `<leader>-` / `<leader>=`: Cycle to previous/next buffer
- `<C-,>` / `<C-.>`: Move buffer one position left/right

The tab bar also shows modification state (`[+]` means unsaved
changes) and Git status indicators.

## 7. Window Operations *windows*

When you have multiple splits, navigate and resize windows quickly:

**Window navigation:** Press `<C-w>c` then `h`/`j`/`k`/`l` to move
to left/down/up/right window. This uses Hydra mode — press `<C-w>c`
once, then chain directional keys without repeating the prefix.

**Window resizing:** Press `<C-w>W` then `h`/`j`/`k`/`l` to resize
windows (`h` decreases width, `l` increases width, `j` increases
height, `k` decreases height).

NOTE: Hydra lets you chain multiple operations after a single prefix
key — press Escape to exit the Hydra. Perfect for heavy window
switching sessions.

## 8. Floating Terminal *terminal*

Run shell commands inside Neovim without switching to an external
terminal:

- `<C-x>ft`: Toggle floating terminal
- `<leader>lj`: Open jjui (Jujutsu version control interface)

The floating terminal opens in the center of the current window.
After running commands, press `jj` to exit insert mode, then
`<C-x>ft` to close it.

TIP: This config uses Jujutsu (`jj`) for version control. Press
`<leader>lj` to open the jjui graphical interface — more intuitive
than the command line for many operations.

## 9. Outline (Symbol Outline) *outline*

Press `<F3>` to toggle the symbol outline panel. It parses functions,
classes, variables, and other symbols in the current file, displayed
in a hierarchical view.

- Use `j`/`k` to select a symbol
- Press Enter to jump to its definition
- Press `<F3>` again to close

Great for quickly grasping a large file's structure or locating a
specific function in a long file.

## 10. Session Management *sessions*

Sessions save and restore your workspace state — open files, window
layout, cursor positions, and more:

- `<leader>ss`: Save the current session (prompt for a name)
- `<leader>sl`: Load a saved session
- `<leader>sd`: Delete a session you no longer need

**Recommended workflow:** Press `<leader>ss` before leaving work,
then `<leader>sl` the next morning to restore everything instantly —
every file is right where you left it.

## 11. Summary: Daily Best-Practice Flow *summary*

Here's a natural workflow after opening a project:

~~~
1. Open project      nvim .
2. Browse files      <F4> Neotree or <C-x><C-f> mini.files
3. Search files      <C-f> Telescope, type filename
4. Switch files      <S-h> / <S-l> back and forth
5. Arrange layout    <C-w>c + h/j/k/l to navigate windows
6. Run commands      <C-x>ft floating terminal
7. View structure    <F3> Outline
8. Save state        <leader>ss save session
~~~

With these skills, you can navigate and manage project files
efficiently. Next, try **Tutorial 03: Efficient Editing** to learn
Flash jumping, multi-cursor editing, and text manipulation
techniques.
